L20|But look at what the two companies were actually selling. Apple sold a computer that fits in a pocket. RIM sold the best way to answer email.
L21|A computer needs the whole front of the phone to be screen. A keyboard needs the front of the phone to be keyboard.
L22|RIM could not have both. Every phone that kept the keyboard gave up screen, and everything a screen was for.
L23|Web pages, maps, video, photographs. A phone with a keyboard through the middle was a worse place to look at anything.
L24|That mattered more every year, because the software people wanted was not on the phone. It was in the app store.
L25|Developers build for the biggest screen and the biggest audience. BlackBerry had neither.
L26|RIM tried. In two thousand eight it released the Storm, its first touchscreen BlackBerry. It sold, and reviewers hated it.
L27|In twenty ten it announced a tablet, the PlayBook. It shipped late, half finished, and it flopped.
L28|After nine hundred thousand units it wrote off four hundred and eighty-five million dollars of stock it could not sell.
